= #text("Help Your Child to Wonder / 帮助孩子保有惊奇")

#figure(image("assets/F001.jpg", width: 85%))

#html.p(id: "en-S001")[#text("One stormy autumn night when my nephew Roger was about twenty months old I wrapped him in a blanket and carried him down to the beach in the rainy darkness. Out there, just at the edge of where-we-couldn’t see, big waves were thundering in, dimly seen white shapes that boomed and shouted and threw great handfuls of froth at us. Together we laughed for pure joy—he a baby,  meeting for the first time the wild tumult of Oceanus, I with the salt of half a lifetime of sea-love in me. But I think we felt the same spine-tingling response to the vast, roaring ocean and the wild night around us.")]

#zh(id: "S001")[#text("一个暴风雨的秋夜，当我的侄子罗杰大约二十个月大的时候，我把他裹在毯子里，在下雨的黑暗中把他带到海滩上。在那里，就在我们看不见的地方的边缘，大浪在打雷，隐约看到白色的形状，蓬勃发展，喊叫，向我们扔了大量的泡沫。我们一起为纯粹的快乐而笑 -- 他是个婴儿，第一次遇到大洋的狂野骚动，我带着半辈子海爱的盐。但是我认为我们对周围广阔，咆哮的海洋和狂野的夜晚感到同样的刺痛。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S002")[#text("A night or two later the storm had blown itself out and I took Roger again to the beach, this time to carry him along the water’s edge, piercing the darkness with the yellow cone of our flashlight. Although there was no rain the night was again noisy with breaking waves and the insistent wind. It was clearly a time and place where great and elemental things prevailed.")]

#zh(id: "S002")[#text("一两个晚上后，暴风雨把自己吹了出来，我又把罗杰带到海滩，这次是把他带到水边，用手电筒的黄色锥体刺穿黑暗。尽管没有下雨，夜晚依旧被拍岸的浪涛声和持续不断的风声所喧嚣。显然，这是一个伟大而基本的事物盛行的时间和地点。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S003")[#text("Our adventure on this particular night had to do with life, for we were searching for ghost crabs, those sand-colored, fleet-legged beings which Roger had sometimes glimpsed briefly on the beaches in daytime. But the crabs are chiefly nocturnal, and when not roaming the night beaches they dig little pits near the surf line where they hide, seemingly watching and waiting for what the sea may bring them. For me the sight of these small living creatures, solitary and fragile against the brute force of the sea, had moving philosophic overtones, and I do not pretend that Roger and I reacted with similar emotions. But it was good to see his infant acceptance of a world of elemental things, fearing neither the song of the wind nor the darkness nor the roaring surf, entering with baby excitement into the search for a “ghos.”")]

#zh(id: "S003")[#text("在这个特殊的夜晚，我们的冒险与生活有关，因为我们正在寻找幽灵蟹，那些沙色的，舰队腿的生物，罗杰有时白天在海滩上短暂瞥见。但是螃蟹主要是夜间活动的，当它们不在夜间海滩上漫游时，它们会在隐藏的冲浪线附近挖一些小坑，似乎在注视并等待大海可能带给它们的东西。对我来说，这些小生物的景象，孤独而脆弱，对抗大海的蛮力，有移动的哲学色彩，我不假装罗杰和我的反应是相似的情绪。但是，很高兴看到他的婴儿接受了一个元素世界，既不害怕风的歌声，也不害怕黑暗，也不害怕咆哮的海浪，带着婴儿的兴奋进入寻找 “ghos” 的过程。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S004")[#text("It was hardly a conventional way to entertain one so young, I suppose, but now, with Roger a little past his fourth birthday, we are continuing that sharing of adventures in the world of nature that we began in his babyhood, and I think the results are good. The sharing includes nature in storm as well as calm, by nights as well as by day, and is based on having fun together rather than on teaching.")]

#zh(id: "S004")[#text("我想，这几乎不是一种传统的娱乐方式，但是现在，罗杰已经过了四岁生日，我们继续分享我们从他的婴儿时代开始的自然世界的冒险经历，我认为结果很好。分享包括风暴中的自然以及平静，夜晚和白天，并且基于一起玩乐而不是教学。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S005")[#text("I spend the summer months on the coast of Maine, where I have my own shoreline and my own small tract of woodland. Bayberry and juniper and huckleberry begin at the very edge of the granite rim of shore, and where the land slopes upward from the bay in a wooded knoll the air becomes fragrant with spruce and balsam. Underfoot there is the multi-patterned northern ground cover of blueberry, checkerberry, reindeer moss and bunchberry, and on a hillside of many spruces, with shaded ferny dells and rocky outcroppings—called the Wildwoods— there are lady’s-slippers and wood lilies and the slender wants of clintonia with its deep blue berries.")]

#zh(id: "S005")[#text("我在缅因州的海岸度过了夏季，在那里我有自己的海岸线和自己的一小块林地。杨梅，杜松和哈克贝利从海岸花岗岩边缘的最边缘开始，在一片树木繁茂的小山中，土地从海湾向上倾斜，空气中弥漫着云杉和香脂的芬芳。脚下有蓝莓，棋盘式浆果，驯鹿苔藓和bunchberry的多图案北部地面覆盖物，在许多云杉的山坡上，带有阴影的蕨类植物和岩石露头-称为野林-有女士拖鞋和木百合花，还有细长的clintonia及其深蓝色浆果。")]

#figure(image("assets/F002.jpg", width: 85%), caption: [#text("A three-year-old can love the song of the wind, speeding clouds, the roaring surf. A baby chick feels throbbingly soft and warm and little and lovable in the hand.") #zh-caption[#text("三岁的孩子也会喜爱风的歌声、疾驰的云朵和咆哮的海浪。小鸡在手心里微微悸动，柔软、温暖，小小的，惹人怜爱。")]])

#html.p(id: "en-S006")[#text("When Roger has visited me in Maine and we have walked in these woods I have made no conscious effort to name plants or animals nor to explain to him, but have just expressed my own pleasure in what we see, calling his attention to this or that but only as I would share discoveries with an older person. Later I have been amazed at the way names stick in his mind, for when I show color slides of my woods plants it is Roger who can identify them. “Oh, that’s what Rachel likes—that bunchberry!” Or, “That’s Jumer (juniper) but you can’t eat those green berries—they are for the squirrels.” I am sure no amount of drill would have implanted the names so firmly as just going through the woods in the spirit of two friends on an expedition of exciting discovery.")]

#zh(id: "S006")[#text("当罗杰在缅因州拜访我，我们在这些树林里散步时，我没有有意识地努力给植物或动物命名，也没有向他解释，但只是表达了我自己对我们所看到的感到高兴，呼吁他注意这个或那个，但只是因为我会与一个年长的人分享发现。后来，我对名字在他脑海中的方式感到惊讶，因为当我展示我的树林植物的彩色幻灯片时，罗杰可以识别它们。“哦，这就是瑞秋喜欢的 -- 那个bunchberry!” 或者，“那是Jumer (杜松)，但你不能吃那些绿色的浆果 -- 它们是给松鼠吃的。” 我敢肯定，再多的钻探也不会像两个朋友在一次令人兴奋的发现探险中穿越树林那样牢牢地植入这些名字。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S007")[#text("In the same way Roger learned the shells on my little triangle of sand that passes for a beach in rocky Maine. When he was only a year and a half old, they became known to him as winkies (periwinkles), weks (whelks) and mukkies (mussels) without my knowing quite now this came about, for I had not tried to teach him.")]

#zh(id: "S007")[#text("罗杰以同样的方式学习了我的小三角形沙子上的贝壳，这些贝壳经过了洛基缅因州的海滩。当他只有一岁半的时候，他们被他称为winkies (长春花)，weks (海螺) 和mukkies (贻贝)，而我现在却不知道，因为我没有试图教他。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S008")[#text("We have let Roger share our enjoyment of things people ordinarily deny children because they are inconvenient, interfering with bedtime, or involving wet clothing that has to be changed or mud that has to be cleaned off the rug.")]

#zh(id: "S008")[#text("我们让罗杰分享我们对人们通常拒绝孩子的事情的享受，因为他们不方便，干扰就寝时间，或者涉及必须更换的湿衣服或必须清除地毯上的泥土。")]

#figure(image("assets/F003.jpg", width: 85%), caption: [#text("A wriggly lizard beguiles endlessly. In the sand dwell secret things like ghost crabs, which live in little pits at the edge of the sea. A boy can sit forever in the silent woods, finding the world mirrored in a still pool. Children delight in small things because they are closer to the ground than we. The quiet woods path, carpeted in silvery green, feels deep and springy on a rainy day. Up on the sand dunes, stinging hot, it seems like the very edge of the world.") #zh-caption[#text("一条扭动的蜥蜴，足以让孩子久久着迷。沙滩里住着神秘的小生灵，例如在海边小洞穴里栖身的沙蟹。男孩能在寂静的树林里坐上许久，从一汪静水中看见世界的倒影。孩子喜欢微小的事物，因为他们比我们更贴近地面。幽静的林间小路铺着银绿色的地毯，雨天踩上去厚实而富有弹性。沙丘灼热刺人，置身其上，仿佛来到了世界的尽头。")]])

#html.p(id: "en-S009")[#text("We have let him join us in the dark living room before the big picture window to watch the full moon riding lower and lower toward the far shore of the bay, setting all the water ablaze with silver flames and finding a thousand diamonds in the rocks on the shore as the light strikes the flakes of mica embedded in them. I think we have felt that the memory of such a scene, photographed year after year by his child’s mind, would mean more to him in manhood than the sleep he was losing. He told me it would, in his own way, when we had a full moon the night after his arrival last summer. He sat quietly on my lap for some time, watching the moon and the water and all the night sky, and then he whispered, “I’m glad we came.”")]

#zh(id: "S009")[#text("我们让他和我们一起在大窗户前的黑暗客厅里，手表满月越来越低地向海湾的远岸骑行，用银色的火焰点燃所有的水，在岸边的岩石上发现一千颗钻石，因为光线照射在嵌入其中的云母薄片上。我想我们已经感觉到，对这样一个场景的记忆，年复一年地由他的孩子的头脑拍摄，对他来说，比他失去的睡眠更重要。他告诉我，以他自己的方式，当我们有一个满月在他去年夏天到来后的晚上。他静静地坐在我的腿上一段时间，看着月亮和水和整个夜空，然后他低声说，“我很高兴我们来了。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S010")[#text("A rainy day is the perfect time for a walk in the woods. I always thought so myself; the Maine woods never seem so fresh and alive as in wet weather. Then all the needles on the evergreens wear a sheath of silver; ferns seems to have grown to almost tropical lushness and every leaf has its edging of crystal drops. Strangely colored fungi—mustard-yellow and apricot and scarlet—are pushing out of the leaf mold and all the lichens and the mosses have come alive with green and silver freshness.")]

#zh(id: "S010")[#text("雨天正是在林间漫步的绝佳时机。我自己也一直这么想; 缅因州的树林从来没有像潮湿的天气那样新鲜活泼。然后，常绿植物上的所有针都戴着银鞘; 蕨类植物似乎已经长到几乎热带的茂美，每片叶子都有晶滴的边缘。奇怪的彩色真菌-芥末-黄色，杏和猩红色-从叶子霉菌中推出，所有的地衣和苔藓都充满了绿色和银色的新鲜感。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S011")[#text("Now I know that for children, too, nature reserves some of her choice rewards for days when her mood may appear to be somber .Roger reminded me of it on a long walk through rain-drenched woods last summer—not in words, of course, but by his responses. There had been rain and fog for days, rain beating on the big picture window, fog almost shutting out sight of the bay. No lobstermen coming in to tend their traps, no gulls on the shore, scarcely even a squirrel to watch. The cottage was fast becoming too small for a restless three-year-old.")]

#zh(id: "S011")[#text("现在我知道，对于孩子们来说，当她的心情看起来很阴沉的时候，大自然也会保留一些她选择的奖励。去年夏天，罗杰在雨水弥漫的树林中漫步时，让我想起了这件事 -- 当然不是用语言，而是用他的回答。几天来一直有雨和雾，雨打在大窗户上，雾几乎把海湾拒之门外。没有龙虾来照看他们的陷阱，岸上没有海鸥，甚至连一只松鼠也没有手表。对于一个不安分的三岁孩子来说，小屋很快变得太小了。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S012")[#text("“Let’s go for a walk in the woods,” I said. “Maybe we’ll see a fox or a deer.” So into yellow oilskin coat and sou’wester and outside in joyous anticipation.")]

#zh(id: "S012")[#text("“我们去树林里散散步吧，”我说。“也许我们会看到一只狐狸或一只鹿。” 于是穿上黄色的油皮大衣，在外面欢快地期待着。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S013")[#text("Having always loved the lichens because they have a quality of fairyland— silver rings on a stone, odd little forms like bones or horns or the shell of a sea creature—I was glad to find Roger noticing and responding to the magic change in their appearance wrought by the rain. The woods path was carpeted with the so- called reindeer moss, in reality a lichen. Like an old-fashioned hall runner, it made a narrow strip of silvery gray through the green of the woods, here and there spreading out to cover a larger area. In dry weather the lichen carpet seems thin; it is brittle and crumbles underfoot. Now, saturated with rain which it absorbs like a sponge, it was deep and springy. Roger delighted in its texture, getting down on chubby knees to feel it, and running from one patch to another to jump up and down in the deep, resilient carpet with squeals of pleasure.")]

#zh(id: "S013")[#text("我一直很喜欢地衣，因为它们有仙境的品质 -- 石头上的银环，奇怪的小形状，比如骨头、角或海洋生物的壳 -- 我很高兴发现罗杰注意到并回应了它们被雨改变的外表。树林小径上铺着所谓的驯鹿苔藓，实际上是地衣。就像老式的hall runner一样，它在树林的绿色中形成了一条银灰色的窄条，到处散布以覆盖更大的区域。在干燥的天气中，地衣地毯似乎很薄; 它很脆，在脚下碎裂。如今，它饱吸雨水，宛如一块海绵，既深又富有弹性。罗杰对它的质地感到高兴，坐在丰满的膝盖上感受它，从一个补丁跑到另一个补丁，在深深的弹性地毯上跳来跳去，伴随着快乐的尖叫声。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S014")[#text("It was here that we first played our Christmas tree game. There is a fine crop of young spruces coming along and one can find seedlings of almost any size down to the length of Roger’s finger. I began to point out the baby trees. “The one must be a Christmas tree for the squirrels,” I would say. “It’s just the right height. On Christmas Eve the red squirrels come and hang little shells and cones and silver threads of lichen on it for ornaments, and then the snow falls and covers it with shining stars, and in the morning the squirrels have a beautiful Christmas tree…And this one is even tinier—it must be for the little bugs of some kinds—and maybe this bigger one is for the rabbits or the woodchucks.”")]

#zh(id: "S014")[#text("就是在这里，我们第一次玩了我们的圣诞树游戏。有很多年轻的云杉，人们可以找到几乎任何大小的幼苗，直到罗杰手指的长度。我开始指着那些小树。“那一定是松鼠的圣诞树，” 我会说。“高度正好。”在圣诞节前夕，红松鼠来了，在上面挂上小贝壳，圆锥体和地衣的银线作为装饰品，然后雪落下，并用闪亮的星星覆盖它，早晨，松鼠有一棵美丽的圣诞树…… 而这棵甚至更小 -- 它一定是给某些小虫子的 -- 也许这棵更大的是给兔子或土拨鼠的。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S015")[#text("Once this game was started it had to be played on all woods walks, which from now on were punctuated by shouts of, “Don’t step on the Christmas tree!”")]

#zh(id: "S015")[#text("一旦这个游戏开始，它必须在所有的伍兹步道上播放，从现在开始，喊着 “不要踩在圣诞树上!”")]

#html.p(id: "en-S016")[#text("A child’s world is fresh and new and beautiful, full of wonder and excitement. It is our misfortune that for most of us that clear-eyed vision, that true instinct for what is beautiful and awe-inspiring, is dimmed and even lost before we reach adulthood. If I had influence with the good fairy who is supposed to preside over the christening of all children I should ask that her gift to each child in the world be a sense of wonder so indestructible that it would last throughout life, as an unfailing antidote against the boredom and disenchantments of later years, the sterile preoccupation with things that are artificial, the alienation from the sources of our strength")]

#zh(id: "S016")[#text("孩子的世界清新而美好，充满着好奇与惊喜。不幸的是，对于我们大多数人来说，清晰的视野，对美丽和令人敬畏的事物的真正本能，在我们成年之前就变得黯淡甚至迷失了。如果我对那个应该主持所有孩子洗礼的好仙女有影响，我应该要求她给世界上每个孩子的礼物是一种坚不可摧的惊奇感，它将持续一生，作为对晚年无聊和失望的坚定解毒剂，对人为事物的无休止的关注，对我们力量来源的疏远")]

#html.p(id: "en-S017")[#text("If a child is to keep alive his inborn sense of wonder without any such gift from the fairies, he needs the companionship of at least one adult who can share it, rediscovering with him the joy, excitement and mystery of the world we live in.")]

#zh(id: "S017")[#text("如果一个孩子要在没有仙女的任何礼物的情况下保持他与生俱来的惊奇感，他需要至少一个可以分享它的成年人的陪伴，与他一起重新发现我们生活的世界的欢乐，兴奋和神秘。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S018")[#text("Parents often have a sense of inadequacy when confronted on the one hand with the eager, sensitive mind of a child and on the other with a world of complex physical nature, inhabited by a life so various and unfamiliar that it seems hopeless to reduce it to order and knowledge. In a mood of self-defeat, they exclaim, “How can I possibly teach my child about nature—why, I don’t even know one bird from another.”")]

#zh(id: "S018")[#text("当父母一方面面对孩子的渴望，敏感的头脑，另一方面面对一个复杂的物理性质的世界时，父母常常会感到不足，因为生活如此多样化和陌生，以至于将其减少到秩序和知识似乎是无望的。在一种自我否定的情绪中，他们感叹道：“我怎么可能教孩子认识大自然呢——连哪种鸟是哪种我都分不清。”")]

#html.p(id: "en-S019")[#text("I sincerely believe that for the child, and for the parent seeking to guide him, it is not half so important to know as to feel. If facts are the seeds that later produce knowledge and wisdom, then the emotions and the impressions of the senses are the fertile soil in which the seeds must grow. The years of early childhood are the time to prepare the soil. Once the emotions have been aroused—a sense of the beautiful, the excitement of the new and the unknown, a feeling of sympathy, pity, admiration or love—then we wish for knowledge about the object of our emotional response. Once found, it has lasting meaning. It is more important to pave the way for the child to want to know than to put him on a diet of facts he is not ready to assimilate.")]

#zh(id: "S019")[#text("我由衷地认为，对于孩子而言，对于试图引导他的父母而言，感受远比知晓更为重要。如果说事实是日后孕育知识与智慧的种子，那么情感与感官印象便是这些种子赖以生长的沃土。幼儿时期是为未来奠定基础的阶段。一旦情感被唤起 -- 一种美丽的感觉，一种新的和未知的兴奋，一种同情，怜悯，钦佩或爱的感觉 -- 那么我们希望了解我们情绪反应的对象。一旦被发现，它便具有永恒的意义。比起让孩子囫囵吞枣地接受他尚无法消化的事实，更重要的是为他铺就一条渴望求知的道路。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S020")[#text("If you are a parent who feels he has little nature lore at his disposal there is still much you can do for your child. With him, wherever you are and whatever your resources, you can still look up at the sky—its dawn and twilight beauties, its moving clouds, its stars by night. You can listen to the wind, whether it blows with majestic voice through a forest or sings a many-voiced chorus around the eaves of your house or the corners of your apartment building, and in the listening, you can gain magical release for your thoughts. You can still feel the rain on your face and think of its long journey, its many transmutations, from sea to air to earth. Even if you are a city dweller, you can find some place, perhaps a park or a golf course, where you can observe the mysterious migrations of the birds and the changing seasons. And with your child you can ponder the mystery of a growing seed, even if it be only one planted in a pot of earth in the kitchen window.")]

#zh(id: "S020")[#text("如果你是一个父母，觉得他几乎没有什么自然知识可以支配，你仍然可以为你的孩子做很多事情。有了他，无论你在哪里，无论你的资源如何，你仍然可以仰望天空 -- 它的黎明和暮色的美丽，它移动的云彩，它的夜晚的星星。您可以聆听风，无论是吹过森林的雄伟声音，还是在房屋的屋檐或公寓楼的角落里唱着许多声音的合唱，在聆听中，您都可以为自己的思想获得神奇的释放。你仍然可以感觉到雨在你的脸上，想想它漫长的旅程，它的许多嬗变，从海洋到空气再到地球。即使您是城市居民，也可以找到一些地方，也许是公园或高尔夫球场，在那里您可以观察鸟类的神秘迁徙和季节的变化。和你的孩子一起，你可以思考一个不断增长的种子的奥秘，即使它只是一个种植在厨房窗户里的一盆土里。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S021")[#text("Exploring nature with your child is largely a matter of becoming receptive to what lies all around you. It is learning again to use your eyes, ears, nostrils and finger tips, opening up the disused channels of sensory impression.")]

#zh(id: "S021")[#text("与您的孩子一起探索自然在很大程度上是接受周围事物的问题。再次学习使用您的眼睛，耳朵，鼻孔和指尖，打开废弃的感官印象通道。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S022")[#text("For most of us, knowledge of our world comes largely through sight, yet we look about with such unseeing eyes that we are partially blind. One way to open your eyes to unnoticed beauty is to ask yourself, “What if I had never seen this before? What if I knew I would never see it again?”")]

#zh(id: "S022")[#text("对于我们大多数人来说，对我们世界的了解很大程度上来自视觉，但我们用看不见的眼睛看周围，以至于我们部分失明。睁开眼睛看到未被注意的美丽的一种方法是问自己，“如果我以前从未见过这个怎么办？如果我知道我再也见不到它了呢？”")]

#html.p(id: "en-S023")[#text("I remember a summer night when such a thought came to me strongly. It was a clear night without a moon. With a friend, I went out on a flat headland that is almost a tiny island, being all but surrounded by the waters of the bay. There the horizons are remote and distant rims on the edge of space. We lay and looked up at the sky and the millions of stars that blazed in darkness. The night was so still that we could hear the buoy on the ledges out beyond the mouth of the bay. Once or twice a word spoken by someone on the far shore was carried across on the clear air. A few lights burned in cottages. Otherwise there was no reminder of other human life; my companion and I were alone with the stars. I have never seen them more beautiful: the misty river of the Milky Way flowing across the sky, the patterns of the constellations standing out bright and clear, a blazing planet low on the horizon. Once or twice a meteor burned its way into the earth’s atmosphere. It occurred to me that if this were a sight that could be seen only once in a century or even once in a human generation, this little headland would be thronged with spectators. But it can be seen many scores of nights in any year, and so the lights burned in the cottages and the inhabitants probably gave not a thought to the beauty overhead; and because they could see it almost any night perhaps they will never see it. An experience like that, when one’s thoughts are released to roam through the lonely spaces of the universe, can be shared with a child even if you don’t know the name of a single star. You can still drink in the beauty, and think and wonder at the meaning of what you see.")]

#zh(id: "S023")[#text("我记得一个夏天的夜晚，我强烈地想到了这样一个想法。那是一个晴朗的无月之夜。我和一个朋友一起去了一个平坦的岬角，几乎是一个小岛，几乎被海湾水域包围。那里的地平线是遥远而遥远的边缘空间。我们躺着，仰望天空和在黑暗中闪耀的数百万颗星星。夜晚是如此的静止，以至于我们可以听到海湾口以外的壁架上的浮标。有一两次远岸的人说的话在晴朗的空气中传过。小屋里有几盏灯烧着。否则，就不会有其他人类生活的回忆; 我和我的同伴独自与星星在一起。我从未见过它们比这更美丽: 银河的雾蒙蒙的河流划过天空，星座的图案明亮而清晰地突出，地平线上有一颗炽热的行星。一次或两次流星燃烧到地球的大气层。我突然想到，如果这是一个只能在一个世纪甚至人类一代中看到的景象，那么这个小小的岬角将挤满了观众。但是在任何一年里都可以看到许多夜晚，所以小屋里的灯光燃烧着，居民们可能没有想到头顶上的美丽; 因为他们几乎在任何一个晚上都能看到它，也许他们永远也看不到它。这样的经历，当一个人的思想被释放，在宇宙的孤独空间中漫游时，即使你不知道一颗恒星的名字，也可以与孩子分享。你仍然可以在美丽中喝酒，思考并想知道您所看到的含义。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S024")[#text("And then there is the world of little things, seen all too seldom. Many children, perhaps because they themselves are small and closer to the ground than we, notice and delight in the small and inconspicuous. With this beginning, it is easy to share with them the beauties we usually miss because we look too hastily, seeing the whole and not its parts. Some of nature’s most exquisite handiwork is on a miniature scale, as anyone knows who has applied a magnifying glass to a snowflake.")]

#zh(id: "S024")[#text("然后是小东西的世界，很少见到。许多孩子，也许是因为他们本身很小，比我们更接近地面，注意到并喜欢小而不显眼的东西。有了这个开始，很容易与他们分享我们通常错过的美丽，因为我们看起来太匆忙，看到的是整体而不是部分。自然界中一些最精致的手工制品是微型的，因为任何人都知道谁在雪花上使用了放大镜。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S025")[#text("An investment of a few dollars in a good hand lens or magnifying glass will bring a new world into being. With your child, look at objects you take for granted as commonplace or uninteresting. A sprinkling of sand grains may appear as gleaming jewels of rose or crystal hue, or as glittering jet beads, or as a mélange of Lilliputian rocks, spines of sea urchins and bits of snail shells.")]

#zh(id: "S025")[#text("只需投资几美元购买一款优质的手持放大镜，就能开启一个全新的世界。和您的孩子一起观察那些您习以为常、认为平凡或无趣的物品。撒上的沙粒可能会像闪闪发光的玫瑰或水晶般的珠宝，闪闪发光的喷射珠，或者是小人国岩石的混合物，海胆的刺和蜗牛壳的碎片。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S026")[#text("A lens-aided view into a patch of moss reveals a dense tropical jungle, in which insects large as tigers prowl amid strangely formed, luxuriant trees. A bit of pond weed or seaweed put in a glass container and studied under a lens is found to be populated by hordes of strange beings, whose activities can entertain you for hours. Flowers (especially the composites), the early buds of leaf or flower from any tree, or any small creature reveal unexpected beauty and complexity when, aided by a lens, we can escape the limitations of the human size scale.")]

#zh(id: "S026")[#text("通过镜头辅助观察一片苔藓，可以看到一片茂密的热带丛林，其中像老虎一样大的昆虫在奇特的茂密树木中徘徊。将一些池塘杂草或海藻放在玻璃容器中并在镜头下进行研究，发现成群结队的奇怪生物居住，它们的活动可以使您娱乐数小时。花朵 (尤其是复合材料)，任何树木或任何小动物的叶子或花朵的早期芽，在镜头的帮助下，我们可以摆脱人类大小尺度的限制，从而展现出意想不到的美丽和复杂性。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S027")[#text("Senses other than sight can prove avenues of delight and discovery, storing up for us memories and impressions. Already Roger and I, out early in the morning, have enjoyed the sharp, clean smell of wood smoke coming from the cottage chimney. Down on the shore we have savored the smell of low tide—that marvelous evocation combined of many separate odors, of the world of seaweeds and fishes and creatures of bizarre shape and habit, of tides rising and falling of their appointed schedule, of exposed mud flats and salt rime drying on the rocks. I hope Roger will later experience, as I do, the rush of remembered delight that comes with the first breath of that scent, drawn into one’s nostrils as one returns to the sea after a long absence. For the sense of smell, almost more than any other, has the power to recall memories and it is a pity that we use it so little.")]

#zh(id: "S027")[#text("除了视觉以外的其他感官也能成为愉悦与发现的途径，为我们积淀下珍贵的记忆与印象。罗杰和我已经在清晨出门，享受着从小屋烟囱里冒出来的尖锐，干净的木烟味。在岸上，我们品尝了退潮的气味-奇妙的唤起结合了许多不同的气味，海藻，鱼类和奇异形状和习性的生物的世界，潮汐的上升和下降，裸露的泥滩和盐雾在岩石上干燥。我希望罗杰以后能像我一样，体验到那种气味的第一次呼吸所带来的回忆般的喜悦，当一个人在久违后回到大海时，他的鼻孔就被吸引住了。对于嗅觉，几乎比其他任何东西都有能力回忆起记忆，可惜我们很少使用它。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S028")[#text("Hearing can be a source of even more exquisite pleasure but it requires conscious cultivation. I have had people tell me they had never heard the song of a wood thrush, although I knew the bell-like phrases of this bird had been ringing in their back yards every spring. By suggestion and example, I believe children can be helped to hear the many voices about them. Take time to listen and talk about the voices of the earth and what they mean—the majestic voice of thunder, the winds, the sound of surf or flowing streams.")]

#zh(id: "S028")[#text("听觉可以成为一种更为精妙的愉悦之源，但这需要有意识地加以培养。有人告诉我，他们从未听过画眉的歌声，尽管我知道每年春天这只鸟的钟声在他们的后院响起。我相信，通过引导和示范，我们可以帮助孩子们倾听围绕在他们身边的各种声音。花时间倾听和谈论地球的声音及其含义-雷声，风，冲浪声或溪流的雄伟声音。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S029")[#text("And the voices of living things: No child should grow up unaware of the dawn chorus of the birds in spring. He will never forget the experience of a specially planned early rising and going out in the predawn darkness. The first voices are heard before daybreak. It is easy to pick out these first, solitary singers. Perhaps a few cardinals are uttering their clear, rising whistles, like someone calling a dog. Then the song of a whitethroat, pure and ethereal, with the dreamy quality of remembered joy. Off in some distant patch of woods a whippoorwill continues his monotonous night chant, rhythmic and insistent, sound that is felt almost more than heard. Robins, thrushes, song sparrows, jays, vireos add their voices. The chorus picks up volume as more and more robins join in, contributing a fierce rhythm of their own that soon becomes dominant in the wild medley of voices. In that dawn chorus one hears the throb of life itself.")]

#zh(id: "S029")[#text("还有生物的声音: 没有孩子长大后不知道春天鸟儿的黎明合唱。他永远不会忘记在黎明前的黑暗中特别计划的早起和外出的经历。黎明前听到第一个声音。很容易挑出这些第一，孤独的歌手。也许有几位枢机主教正在发出清晰，上升的哨声，就像有人叫狗一样。然后是纯白的歌声，纯净而空灵，具有梦幻般的记忆喜悦。在遥远的树林中，whipporw会继续他单调的夜唱，有节奏且坚持不懈，声音几乎比听到的要多。知更鸟，画眉，歌麻雀，鸟，鸟添加他们的声音。随着越来越多的知更鸟加入，合唱团的音量越来越大，从而贡献出自己的激烈节奏，很快就在狂野的声音中占主导地位。在那黎明的合唱中，人们听到了生命本身的悸动。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S030")[#text("There is other living music. I have already promised Roger that we’ll take our flashlights this fall and go out into the garden to hunt for the insects that play little fiddles in the grass and among the shrubbery and flower borders. The sound of the insect orchestra swells and throbs night after night, from midsummer until autumn ends and the frosty nights make the tiny players stiff and numb, and finally the last note is stilled in the long cold. An hour of hunting out the small musicians by flashlight is an adventure any child would love. It gives him a sense of the night’s mystery and beauty, and of how alive it is with watchful eyes and little, waiting forms.")]

#zh(id: "S030")[#text("还有其他生活音乐。我已经答应过罗杰，我们将在今年秋天带上手电筒，到花园里去寻找那些在草丛中，灌木丛和花丛中演奏小小提琴的昆虫。昆虫管弦乐队的声音一夜又一夜地膨胀和悸动，从仲夏到秋末，寒冷的夜晚使微小的演奏者僵硬和麻木，最后最后一个音符在漫长的寒冷中静止。用手电筒寻找小音乐家的一个小时是任何孩子都会喜欢的冒险。它给了他一种夜晚的神秘和美丽的感觉，以及它是多么充满活力，警惕的眼睛和小，等待的形式。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S031")[#text("The game is to listen, not so much to the full orchestra as to the separate instruments, and to try to locate the players. Perhaps you are drawn, step by step, to a bush from which comes a sweet, high-pitched, endlessly repeated trill. Finally you trace it to a little creature of palest green, with wings as white and insubstantial as moonlight. Or from somewhere along the garden path comes a cheerful, rhythmic chirping, a sound as companionable and homely as a fire crackling on a hearth or a cat’s purr. Shifting your light downward you find a black mole cricket disappearing into his grassy den.")]

#zh(id: "S031")[#text("游戏是听，没有那么多的完整的管弦乐队作为单独的乐器，并试图找到球员。也许您被一步步吸引到灌木丛中，从中发出甜美，高亢，无休止的颤音。最后，您将其追溯到一个淡绿色的小动物，翅膀像月光一样洁白而不坚固。或者从花园小径上的某个地方传来欢快，有节奏的chi叫，声音就像壁炉上的火crack啪声或猫的咕噜声一样令人愉悦和温馨。向下移动光线，您会发现一只黑mole鼠消失在他的草窝中。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S032")[#text("Most haunting of all is one I call the fairy bell ringer. I have never found him. I’m not sure I want to. His voice—and surely he himself—are so ethereal, so delicate, so otherworldly, that he should remain invisible, as he has through all the nights I have searched for him. It is exactly the sound that should come from a bell held in the hand of the tiniest elf, inexpressibly clear and silvery, so faint, so barely-to-be-heard that you hold your breath as you bend closer to the green glades from which the fairy chiming comes.")]

#zh(id: "S032")[#text("最令人难忘的，是我称之为“仙女铃铛手”的那一位。我从未找到过他。我不确定我是否想要。他的声音 -- 当然他自己 -- 是如此飘渺，如此微妙，如此超凡脱俗，他应该保持隐形，就像他在我寻找他的所有夜晚一样。这正是最微小的精灵手中握着的铃铛发出的声音，清晰而银色，如此微弱，几乎听不到，当您弯腰靠近绿色的林间空地时，您屏住呼吸仙女的声音。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S033")[#text("The night is a time, too, to listen for other voices, the calls of bird migrants hurrying northward in spring and southward in autumn. Take your child out on a still October night when there is little wind and find a quiet place away from traffic noises. Then stand very still and listen, projecting your consciousness up into the dark arch of the sky above you. Presently your ears will detect tiny wisps of sound— sharp chirps, sibilant lisps and call notes. They are the voices of bird migrants, apparently keeping in touch by their calls with others of their kind scattered through the sky. I never hear these calls without a wave of feeling that is compounded of many emotions—a sense of lonely distances, a compassionate awareness of small lives controlled and directed by forces beyond volition or denial, a surging wonder at the sure instinct for route and direction that so far has baffled human efforts to explain it.")]

#zh(id: "S033")[#text("夜晚也是聆听其他声音的时候，鸟类移民的呼唤在春季向北奔流，在秋季向南奔流。在一个风平浪静的十月夜晚，带孩子出门，找一个远离车流喧嚣的安静地方。然后静静地站着，倾听，将你的意识投向头顶那片幽暗的穹苍。目前，您的耳朵将检测到细小的声音-尖锐的chi声，嘶哑的lisp声和通话音符。它们是候鸟的鸣声，显然正通过叫声与散布在天空中的同类保持联系。我从来没有听到过这些电话，没有一种由许多情感混合而成的感觉-一种孤独的距离感，一种对小生命的同情意识，这种小生命的控制和指导超出意志或否认的力量，到目前为止，人们对路线和方向的确定本能感到惊讶，这使人类为解释它所做的努力感到困惑。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S034")[#text("If the moon is full and the night skies are alive with the calls of bird migrants, then the way is open for another adventure with your child, if he is old enough to use a telescope or a good pair of binoculars. The sport of watching migrating birds pass across the face of the moon has become popular and even scientifically important in recent years, and it is as good a way as I know to give an older child a sense of the mystery of migration. Seat yourself comfortably and focus your glass on the moon. You must learn patience, for unless you are on a well-traveled highway of migration you may have to wait many minutes before you are rewarded. In the waiting periods you can study the topography of the moon, for even a glass of moderate power reveals enough detail to fascinate a space-conscious child. But sooner or later you should begin to see the birds, lonely travelers in space glimpsed as they pass from darkness into darkness.")]

#zh(id: "S034")[#text("如果月亮已满，夜空在鸟类移民的呼唤中还活着，那么如果您的孩子足够大，可以使用望远镜或一副好双筒望远镜，那么与您的孩子进行另一次冒险的方式就可以了。近年来，观看候鸟穿越月球表面的运动变得流行起来，甚至在科学上很重要，据我所知，这是一种让大孩子了解迁徙奥秘的好方法。请舒适地坐下，将您的望远镜对准月亮。你必须学会耐心，因为除非你在一条旅行的高速公路上，否则你可能需要等待很多分钟才能得到回报。在等待期间，您可以研究月球的地形，因为即使是一杯中等功率的玻璃也可以揭示足够的细节，以吸引具有太空意识的孩子。但迟早，你会开始看见那些鸟儿——在黑暗与黑暗之间匆匆掠过的孤独旅人。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S035")[#text("In all this I have said little about identification of the birds, insects, rocks, stars or any other of the living and nonliving things that share this world with us. Of course it is always convenient to give a name to things that arouse our interest. But that is a separate problem, and one that can be solved by any parent who has a reasonably observant eye and the price of various excellent handbooks that are available in quite inexpensive editions.")]

#zh(id: "S035")[#text("在这一切中，我几乎没有提到鸟类、昆虫、岩石、星星或任何其他与我们分享这个世界的生物和非生物的识别。当然，给引起我们兴趣的事物起个名字总是很方便的。但这是一个单独的问题，任何具有合理观察力的父母都可以解决这个问题，并且价格低廉，价格低廉的各种优秀手册。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S036")[#text("I think the value of the game of identification depends on how you play it. If it becomes an end in itself I count it of little use. It is possible to compile extensive lists of creatures seen and identified without ever once having caught a breath-taking glimpse of the wonder of life. If a child asked me a question that suggested even a faint awareness of the mystery behind the arrival of a migrant sandpiper on the beach of an August morning, I would be far more pleased than by the mere fact that he knew it was a sandpiper and not a plover.")]

#zh(id: "S036")[#text("我认为识别游戏的价值取决于你如何玩它。如果它本身成为目的，我认为它没有什么用处。有可能编制广泛的生物列表，这些生物被看到和识别，而从来没有一次瞥见生命的奇迹。如果一个孩子问我一个问题，甚至暗示了一个8月的早晨移民sand到达海滩背后的奥秘，我会比仅仅因为他知道这是一个sand而不是一个事实而感到高兴。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S037")[#text("What is the value of preserving and strengthening this sense of awe and wonder, this recognition of something beyond the boundaries of human existence? Is the exploration of the natural world just a pleasant way to pass the golden hours of childhood or is there something deeper?")]

#zh(id: "S037")[#text("保持和加强这种敬畏和惊奇感的价值是什么，这种对超越人类生存界限的事物的认识是什么？对自然世界的探索只是一种愉快的方式来度过童年的黄金时光，还是有更深层次的东西？")]

#html.p(id: "en-S038")[#text("I am sure there is something much deeper, something lasting and significant.")]

#zh(id: "S038")[#text("我相信有更深刻的东西，持久而重要的东西。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S039")[#text("Those who dwell, as scientists or laymen, among the beauties and mysteries of the earth are never alone or weary of life. Whatever the vexations or concerns of their personal lives, their thoughts can find paths that lead to inner contentment and to renewed excitement in living. Those who contemplate the beauty of the earth find reserves of strength that will endure as long as life lasts. There is symbolic as well as actual beauty in the migration of the birds, the ebb and flow of the tides, the folded bud ready for the spring. There is something infinitely healing in the repeated refrains of nature— the assurance that dawn comes after nights, and spring after winter.")]

#zh(id: "S039")[#text("那些作为科学家或外行居住在地球的美丽和神秘之中的人永远不会孤独或厌倦生活。无论他们的个人生活有什么烦恼或担忧，他们的思想都能找到通往内心满足和生活中重新兴奋的道路。那些思考地球之美的人找到了力量的储备，只要生命持续下去，这种力量就会持续下去。鸟类的迁徙，潮汐的潮起潮落，为春天准备的折叠芽，都具有象征性和实际的美感。在大自然的反复克制中，有一种无限治愈的东西 -- 保证黎明在夜晚之后到来，春天在冬天之后到来。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S040")[#text("I like to remember the distinguished Swedish oceanographer, Otto Pettersson, who died a few years ago at the age of ninety-three, in full possession of his keen mental powers. His son, also world-famous in oceanography, has related in a recent book how intensely his father enjoyed the world about him. “He was an incurable romantic,” the son wrote, “intensely in love with life and with the mysteries of the cosmos.” When he realized he had not much longer to enjoy the earthly scene, Otto Pettersson said to his son, “What will sustain me in my last moments is an infinite curiosity as to what is to follow.”")]

#zh(id: "S040")[#text("我常常想起杰出的瑞典海洋学家奥托·彼得松。他几年前去世，享年九十三岁，直到最后仍保持着敏锐的心智。他的儿子同样是世界知名的海洋学家，在最近出版的一本书里，讲述了父亲是多么热切地享受着周围的世界。儿子写道：‘他是个无可救药的浪漫主义者，深深地热爱生命，也热爱宇宙的奥秘。’当奥托·彼得松意识到自己已没有多少时间可以欣赏人间景象时，他对儿子说：‘在生命的最后时刻，支撑我的将是对接下来会发生什么的无限好奇。’")]

#html.p(id: "en-S041")[#text("The lasting pleasures of contact with the natural world are not reserved for such scientists but are available to anyone who will place himself under the influence of earth, sea and sky and their amazing life. In my mail recently was a letter that bore eloquent testimony to the lifelong durability of a sense of wonder. It came from a reader who asked advice on choosing a seacoast spot for a vacation, a place wild enough that she might spend her days roaming beaches unspoiled by civilization, exploring that world that is old but ever new. Regretfully she excluded the rugged northern shores. She had loved the shore all her life, she said, but climbing over the rocks of Maine might be difficult, for an eighty-ninth birthday would soon arrive. As I put down her letter I was warmed by the fires of wonder and amazement that still burned brightly in her youthful mind and spirit, just as they must have done fourscore years ago.")]

#zh(id: "S041")[#text("亲近自然世界所带来的持久乐趣，并非只有这样的科学家才能享有；任何愿意让自己置身于大地、海洋、天空及其奇妙生命的影响之中的人，都能得到它。最近收到的一封信，生动地见证了惊奇之心如何能够伴随人的一生。这位读者请我推荐一个适合度假的海滨去处，要足够荒野，让她能够整日在未受文明侵扰的海滩上漫游，探索那个古老却又常新的世界。她遗憾地排除了北方那些崎岖的海岸。她说，自己一生都深爱海滨，但攀爬缅因州的礁石可能会有困难，因为她很快就要迎来八十九岁生日了。放下信时，她那年轻的心灵和精神中仍在明亮燃烧的惊奇与赞叹之火，使我感到温暖；八十年前，那火焰想必也曾这样燃烧。")]
